A1. You will always find a lot of dumb-bells in this class-room. ~ GYMNASIUM
A9. This sounds like the exhaust. ~ GASP
A13. Down in Missouri they consider this one a Saint. ~ LOUIS
A14. A risk watch. ~ SPY
A15. This is what comes from eating too many onions. ~ ODOR
A16. According to the modern subdivisions this would be quite a lot. (.025 of an acre—plural) ~ ARS
A17. This one has been responsible for a lot of follies. ~ FLO
A18. This is very touching. ~ FONDLE
A20. This is a low down definition and is hard to comprehend. ~ DEEP
A22. Here is your chance to get even. ~ EEN
A23. Every afternoon these lie around the house and have a nap. ~ RUGS
A24. What the salesman does to keep the wolf from the door. ~ URGES
A26. A chunk of stock that ought to bring in a fat income. ~ HOG
A27. What a Beta Gamma eta. ~ PI
A28. One of those horrid things she comes down to breakfast in. ~ MOOD
A29. All you have to do to get this is to press the button. (See!) ~ LIGHT
A31. This one has just rented a two-room apartment with all the modern inconveniences. ~ AD
A32. A sign of advancement in the traffic problem. ~ GO
A34. This was started in crossword puzzles. ~ BEGUN
A36. Don't let anyone string you with this. ~ TWINE
A38. Nothing original about this—we got it second-hand. ~ USED
A41. There is no end to these in the circus. ~ RINGS
A43. This team used to have more strength than speed. ~ OXEN
A44. This one has been a hero and then again a fool. ~ ACTOR
A46. This one is usually slammed when he comes to a door. ~ AGENT
A48. The Main thing in every small town (abbr.) ~ ST
A49. Literally speaking this Egyptian god ended Cleopatra. ~ RA
A50. These will probably become movie stars. ~ GOWNS
A52. What a movie fan does at a theatre. ~ AIRS
A55. Not quite so much as Horizontal 16. ~ AR
A56. Bumped. ~ MET
A57. After this it is usually easy. ~ SPEAK
A58. Have you had your iron today? Well here is where the mineral springs (plur.) ~ SPAS
A60. Pan. ~ MAP
A61. This is often lost in the shuffle. ~ HAND
A64. What school boys like to play in good weather. ~ TRUANT
A66. This is about as indefinite as the financial condition. ~ ANY
A67. Rejoice! ye women, this is the end of the stocking. ~ TOE
A68. As father is the only one in the family who has not attended college this is all he can do. ~ EARN
A69. The end of the present year. ~ ONE
A70. Many contests are conducted in the advertising field, but long ago they were held in this field. ~ ARENA
A72. This is the she in sheep (plur.) ~ EWES
A73. What Rudy Vallee has done for the fair sex over the radio. ~ SERENADED

D1. The kind of tidings everyone is waiting to hear from Wall Street. ~ GLAD
D2. "Way back, when"— ~ YORE
D3. A collection of curiosities and antiques. (Don't confuse it with the Senate.) ~ MUSEUM
D4. This is only worth a nickel. ~ NI
D5. Here is where we run out of gas. ~ AS
D6. These are British. ~ ISLES
D7. Above. ~ UPON
D8. This shows much self-possession. ~ MY
D9. This sounds alarming. ~ GONG
